<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khusrau Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1320">1320</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Nasir ud-Din Khusrau Shah, Hasan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khusrau Khan, regnal name Nasir ud-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khusrau-shah">Khusrau Shah</a>, was the sixteenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> for about two months in 1320. Born among the Baradus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> military group, he was captured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>'s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in 1305, brought to Delhi as a slave and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Sultan Mubarak Shah, whose favourite he became, gave him his title and made him vazir; he retook Devagiri in 1317 and forced the Kakatiya ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> to resume tribute in 1318. In 1320 he led Baradus and discontented nobles in killing Mubarak Shah and took the throne, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-tughluq">Ghazi Malik</a>'s rebels defeated him, and he was captured and killed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khusrau-khan.jpg" alt="Khusrau Khan" width="330" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohammed Tariq · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khusru_khilji.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ghiyath al-Din Tughluq, a frontier commander, took the throne in 1320 by defeating and killing <b>Khusrau Khan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
